  "reykjavik.M.list.45.occupation": "Economist, tourism specialist",
  "reykjavik.M.list.46.occupation": "Candidate",
  "reykjavik.M.list.5.bio": "Helgi Dagbjartur Áss Grétarsson was born on 18 February 1977 and is a lawyer and chess grandmaster. He became World Junior Chess Champion in 1994. He has served on Reykjavík City Council since 2022, first as a representative of the Independence Party, but joined the Centre Party in January 2026.",
  "reykjavik.M.list.5.interests.0": "Planning issues and densification policy",
  "reykjavik.M.list.5.interests.1": "The city's efficiency",
  "reykjavik.M.list.5.interests.2": "Chess and sports",
  "reykjavik.M.list.5.occupation": "Deputy City Councillor, chess grandmaster",
  "reykjavik.M.list.6.bio": "Páll Edwald is a lawyer and entrepreneur and works as chief legal counsel at the construction company Reir Verk. He is co-founder and managing director of the dairy company Dóttir Skyr, which has been on the Danish market since late summer 2024 and has sold more than 55 tonnes of skyr there. He has previously worked in the restaurant industry (including as founder of the Ask pizza place in Egilsstaðir), at the law firm LEX, and elsewhere. He is the son of Ari Edwald, former CEO of Mjólkursamsalan, and is in 6th place on the Centre Party list in Reykjavík for the 2026 city council elections.",
  "reykjavik.M.list.6.occupation": "Overlay specialist",
  "reykjavik.M.list.7.occupation": "Businesswoman",
  "reykjavik.M.list.8.occupation": "Education adviser",
  "reykjavik.M.list.9.occupation": "International representative",
  "reykjavik.M.tagline": "Let common sense take over Reykjavík City Hall.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.0.text": "Simplify the City’s systems and strengthen direct democracy so that the voices of residents have a real impact on their immediate surroundings. The purpose of systems should always be to improve people’s lives, not complicate them.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.0.title": "Direct democracy and simpler systems",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.1.text": "Fund the queer youth center (Flotinn); ensure systematic work against hate speech; implement the multicultural policy; ensure that people who do not understand Icelandic can access information and participate in decision-making. Pay particular attention to the situation of older queer people.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.1.title": "Human rights and an inclusive society",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.2.text": "Start from scratch in Reykjavík City’s child protection services — ensure that the chain of responsibility, oversight, and reviews are structured in a transparent and efficient way. A child in need of support should always experience humane services guided by their needs.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.2.title": "A transformation of child protection",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.3.text": "Make it easier for preschool assistants to pursue teacher education alongside work through subsidies. Increase the number of psychologists and speech therapists in schools. Strengthen the arts and vocational subjects in preschools and compulsory schools.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.3.title": "Preschool teachers, psychologists, and arts education",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.4.text": "Fully fund Reykjavík City’s cultural policy. Abolish property taxes on spaces used for cultural activities. Reorganize the City’s grant system so that artists have direct access to allocations through a transparent process. Provide rehearsal spaces for the performing arts, theatre, and emerging musicians.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.4.title": "Culture and the arts: full funding and direct access",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.5.text": "Increase bus frequency to intervals of 7–10 minutes. The goal is for 70% of residents to live within 400 meters of a stop with 10-minute peak-hour service no later than 2040. Continue expanding the cycling path network and ensure more safe walking routes.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.5.title": "Transport: frequency and density",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.6.text": "Implement the newly adopted urban design policy that Píratar brought forward. A human-scale environment, greener areas, and quality development in dense neighborhoods where shops and services are within walking distance. Prioritize the construction of affordable housing that lasts.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.6.title": "Urban design and housing",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.7.text": "A digital service revolution is already delivering annual savings of ISK 1.7 billion — more funding for infrastructure and services. Early intervention in welfare and child protection to prevent bigger problems. Better coordinate the operation of the City’s housing, vehicle fleet, and procurement.",
  "reykjavik.P.agenda.7.title": "A digital service revolution and operations",
  "reykjavik.P.list.1.bio": "Kristinn Jón Ólafsson, known as Kiddi, was born in 1981 and has devoted himself to innovation and digital solutions in the service of the city. He completed an MBA in innovation and technology in 2021. He led the work on Reykjavík’s first innovation policy and digital policy and is chair of the board of Markaðsstofa höfuðborgarsvæðisins.",
  "reykjavik.P.list.1.interests.0": "Innovation and digital services",
  "reykjavik.P.list.1.interests.1": "Environmental issues and green transport systems",
  "reykjavik.P.list.1.interests.2": "A child-friendly city",
  "reykjavik.P.list.1.interests.3": "Transparency and democratic participation",
  "reykjavik.P.list.1.occupation": "Alternate City Councillor (Píratar), lead candidate in Reykjavík",
  "reykjavik.P.list.10.occupation": "Secretary of the Pirates in Reykjavík",
  "reykjavik.P.list.11.occupation": "Teacher",
  "reykjavik.P.list.12.occupation": "Artist",
  "reykjavik.P.list.13.occupation": "Musician, student",
  "reykjavik.P.list.14.occupation": "Director of Múltíkúltí",
  "reykjavik.P.list.15.occupation": "Author, musician",
  "reykjavik.P.list.16.occupation": "Student teacher, President of Trans Ísland",
